Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action; working-class Milwaukee 1990s into early 2000s, then contemporary Seattle. Warm practicals, grain, winter gray.

FADE IN:

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - KIRK STREET - DAY

A cramped brick walk-up. Dust in the light. MA (60s, brown hair gone dull, tired eyes) dozes in a worn chair before a game show. NARRATOR (late 20s, ordinary build, soft face, pizza-shop clothes) hauls grocery bags through the door.

(SOUND: TV game-show buzzer, distant traffic through thin glass)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not that stupid book from junior high. My real Big Brother. Nick.

The door opens again. NICK fills the frame — tall, wrecking-ball presence, reddish-brown hair uncombed, scars on chin and over the left eyebrow, face like a puzzle glued rough. Burnt-orange jacket. He nods once at the bags.

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

NARRATOR
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That's how old people go downhill.

(SOUND: floorboards creak under Nick's boots)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. People moved out of the way.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET - DAY (FLASH)

Kids' kickball. A dented garbage-can lid for a base. LITTLE MARK boots a ball straight through a kitchen window.

(SOUND: glass shattering, distant shout)

Young NICK steps up to furious MRS. ENGEL without flinching.

YOUNG NICK
I did it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never told Ma it was Mark. None of us did. Somehow a twelve-year-old came up with sixty bucks for the glass.

EXT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Through the open door: a lifter mid-preacher-curl sees Nick enter and drops a forty-pound bar with a clang, offering the bench.

(SOUND: iron plates slam, gym echo)

NICK
(nods, takes over)
Fifty. Warm-up.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Milwaukee. Kirk Street. Fifth floor. Vikings, Ma said. Tall people who kept warm when others froze. Nick was a modern-day warrior.

INT. METAL FACTORY FLOOR - DAY

Sparks. A crane lifts steel. Nick runs the line, easy money in his posture.

(SOUND: machinery roar, metal on metal)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nice car. Cool apartment on Werner. He came over a lot. Someone had to stay with Ma. Her eyes were going. Dad took off when I was eight.

EXT. PIZZA PLACE SIDEWALK - DAY

Narrator locks a bike. SIONNA comes toward him — short brown hair, pale English-story skin, light-blue eyes like pre-dawn, black V-neck, fish-symbol necklace, jeans, blue tennis shoes, big backpack. She trips on cement and brushes his shoulder.

(SOUND: bike chain tick, soft impact, street noise)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. They look at each other too long.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Sionna? That's different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon.

He is not really listening — only the eyes.

NARRATOR
Want to come in for pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don't eat pizza. But if you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes a number. Walks away. Looks back over her shoulder as if memorizing him.

(SOUND: her footsteps fade; a bus hisses)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No girl had ever been interested in me. It was like I knew her. Intense. Like waves at the ocean in New Jersey — the only vacation before Dad left. She was washing up on me. I didn't want to move my feet.

INT. APARTMENT KITCHEN - LATE AFTERNOON

Pill bottles lined in a careful row. Ma rummages, glassy-eyed, mumbling.

(SOUND: plastic bottles rattling, fridge hum)

MA
I can't find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR
Ma, we don't go to church anymore. Mrs. Canasta lives one level down. Your pills are right in front of you.

She stares as if she does not know him. Then snaps back.

MA
Oh, right. I am so sorry. I see them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I would have to tell Nick. He would get mad like it was my fault.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Packers-Bears on the TV. Narrator at the bar. Nick slaps his back.

(SOUND: crowd roar from TV, pool balls crack, bar chatter)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

NARRATOR
Ma was all confused. Church. Mrs. Canasta. Empty eyes.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up. Take her to the store. Holy Christ, do something before we pay for a nursing home. I don't have the money for that shit.

Narrator pays, stands. Nick's look could punch.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sometimes I really hate Nick inside. I would never tell anybody that.

EXT. STREET / PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Cold. Narrator crosses toward the glowing library windows, open till nine.

(SOUND: wind, his quick footsteps, library door sigh)

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Old wood floors, chain-hung lights, smell of paper. Narrator at the desk with a temporary card, then in the classic literature stacks. He pulls Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: stamp on card, pages riffling, distant chair scrape)

LIBRARIAN
Plastic one comes in the mail.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when everything was free in this building?

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma already in bed. Narrator reads under a lamp. Heathcliff and Catherine. His eyes close.

(SOUND: pages turning, building pipes tick)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the dream it was Sionna's face in old clothes, horses, moors. I would end up feeling for her like Heathcliff.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College backpacks, glasses, textbooks. Sionna at a back table — loose green hooded top, soft leather shoes, those watery blue eyes. Narrator sits.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low indie music, murmur of study groups)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
I know. I read it.

She talks — ghosts, doubles, the moors, wild infertile land. Love that never changes destroys.

SIONNA
A love that never changes will destroy you.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It reminded me of Nick. Strong and unwavering. Something inside me was coming awake.

SIONNA
Dinner Friday?

He nods, lost in her.

EXT. STREET - DAY

Narrator walks to the bus, thinking.

(SOUND: bus air brakes, city undercurrent)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Maybe that was Ma's problem. Dad left and she never moved on. There ain't no way I could ever forgive him. Nick became my dad when I was eight. I would never do that to a family.

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

He bolts upright, sweating, cold room.

(SOUND: bedsprings, his hard breath, radiator knock)

NARRATOR
Hold!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Forest. Brown mare. Linen sleeves, red tunic. I lifted her down. First kiss. Eyes the lightest blue. It was Sionna in another life. It had to be make-believe. Didn't it?

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats. Twenty people. Narrator in T-shirt and shorts, shirt falling over his face until he tucks it. Awkward down-dog, shaking.

(SOUND: instructor's calm voice, collective breath, floor creaks)

SIONNA
(after class, smiling)
You were really great at that.

NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
You caught on quickly. You liked it, didn't you?

NARRATOR
Yeah. I just felt so dumb.

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. That's why they call it practice.

They smile. He leaves lighter.

INT. PIZZA PLACE - NIGHT

Narrator spins dough. Even the asshole customers do not touch him.

(SOUND: oven doors, order bell, dough slap)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Best night of work ever. Nothing bothered me. Just calm.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

Nick stomps. Ma sits small in her chair. Nick's fists open and close, face red.

(SOUND: door slam echo, TV low)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I was with someone. I met a girl.

NICK
A girl. That's worth all this. Look at your priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Would I want Sionna to meet Nick? He wouldn't understand her. She might be afraid. I was trying to figure how Ma, Nick, Sionna could fit.

INT. LIBRARY STACKS - DAY

Narrator checks out yoga and Buddhism books.

(SOUND: barcode beep, soft aisle footsteps)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Attachment causes suffering. Happiness is not outside. Meditate on the present. Life is suffering — and there is a way out.

INT. APARTMENT LIVING ROOM - DAY

Ma on a game show. Narrator sits with her after calling Sionna.

(SOUND: TV applause, Ma's thin breath)

MA
It feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest. I'm a little dizzy.

He reaches for the phone.

INT. DR. CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

Gray sky in the window. DR. CARSON serious across the desk.

(SOUND: clinic intercom, distant siren)

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. Risk of stroke. Take her to the emergency room. IV meds until she's stabilized.

NARRATOR
I make sure she takes the pills.

DR. CARSON
Confused? Chest pressure this afternoon?

NARRATOR
Yeah. She hasn't been acting normal.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Ma dozing, monitors. Sionna visits; they walk the corridor. Narrator later asleep in the chair.

(SOUND: monitor beep, soft shoes on linoleum)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think I loved her. I just hadn't told her yet.

DREAM - BATTLEFIELD - NIGHT

Chaos, steel, blood. A dying man: "Save her." Nick runs in — and drives a long knife into Narrator's chest.

(SOUND: blades, screaming, then wet silence)

Narrator gasps awake in the hospital chair.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick had killed me. Betrayal by the closest family. Worst of all.

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT

Blue weekly pill container on the table. Sionna helped fill it. Nick enters beet-red, hair wild, boots heavy. Settles in the brown chair.

(SOUND: boots, chair springs, Ma's soft snore from the bedroom)

NICK
Man, you shoulda been a nurse.

NARRATOR
Somebody has to make sure she stays on track. Where have you been?

NICK
Don't start giving ME shit. I was at work. More than you at that bullshit pizza parlor.

Narrator shows the container. Nick smirks.

NICK
Nurse Nancy. How'd you come up with this?

NARRATOR
Sionna helped. She's really smart.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

NARRATOR
Stop it. You haven't even met her.

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

NARRATOR
I'm not a bastard!

NICK
You sure are a giant pussy — and that makes you and me way different.

NARRATOR
You're so goddamn hateful. Was Ma on crack when she was pregnant with you?

WHACK. Nick's hand across Narrator's head. Ear rings. Nick stomps out. Door slams.

(SOUND: slap, ear ring, door crash)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In that split second I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

EXT. SCHOOLYARD - DAY (FLASHBACK - YEARS EARLIER)

Late spring, lilacs. Young Narrator and BOBBY howling with laughter. MRS. STARK advances. Sixth-grade NICK intercepts.

(SOUND: kids yelling, recess bell distant)

MRS. STARK
Mr. Olsen! Explain yourself before I send you to Mr. Pauls.

YOUNG NICK
He didn't mean anything. I'll talk to him. He'll never act up again. I guarantee it.

MRS. STARK
It's too bad your brother doesn't have your level head. This time I'll let it pass.

Later, Nick musses the boy's hair in a small bedroom.

YOUNG NICK
I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It made me love him. It made me hate him. He lived in a bubble. Magic.

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT (MONTHS LATER)

Nick in suede jacket. Ma on a cop show. Narrator mentions community college, business degree, student loan. Sionna's advice.

(SOUND: TV cop siren, keys in basket)

NICK
Going to school? How the hell will you pay? Who watches Ma while you screw around with pencil-pushers?

NARRATOR
I don't want the pizza place my whole life. This was my idea. I need my family on my side. You're my brother.

NICK
How many times have I saved your ass? Donny Amstel in high school — I told him we had mafia connections. He believed me.

Nick softens a fraction, hits Narrator's arm.

NICK
You're not just my brother. You're my best friend. Nothin' comes between us. Not school, not Ma, not this girl. Bros before hos.

Narrator smiles with effort.

NARRATOR
Sure, Nick. I get you.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Smoke, Bob and Sammy, beer. They rag Narrator about being whipped. He calls them blind to class. Later —

INT. JOE'S BAR - POOL ROOM - NIGHT

Packed, AC/DC, smoke. LINDSEY with a dark-haired man. Nick's eyes go crazy. He pushes toward them. Narrator steps in front.

(SOUND: AC/DC from speakers, cue sticks, room going quiet)

NARRATOR
Leave it, man. You don't even know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it. You're next if you don't get out of my face.

NARRATOR
Stop it! You're crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego!

Nick's fist draws back in slow motion toward Narrator's face. JOE and a BOUNCER rush in. Head-butt. Chaos. Narrator runs into the cold night until his chest burns — to Sionna's door.

(SOUND: fist whoosh, shouts, door bang, then only his boots and heartbeat)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Green suede sofa. He tells her everything. She strokes his face.

(SOUND: old house settle, her soft voice)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares does that. He is a thorn. You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. Don't let him stop your purpose.

NARRATOR
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you, and honor it. Stay tonight. Nick is violent right now. Ma is okay for one night.

He sleeps with his head in her lap. Dream: she rises from a lake as an angel; Nick with a mace shrinks and vanishes in her light.

(SOUND: water lapping in dream, then 3 a.m. clock tick)

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

Sionna's peach pie. Ma laughing at old farm stories — first real laugh in years. Knock. Nick walks in. The meeting.

(SOUND: forks on plates, then sudden silence)

SIONNA
I'm Sionna. Nice to meet you. I've heard so much about you.

NICK
(reluctantly shakes)
University lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
English major, philosophy minor. College professor, if I can.

NICK
All that school just doesn't pay off. Harder for a woman.

SIONNA
Bachelor's degrees for women rose from thirty-five to fifty-five percent. We are sixty percent of undergraduates. You can't keep a good woman down. Diversity helps us learn.

She takes plates to the kitchen. Nick stunned, then snide.

NICK
Doctor Sionna. Someone in this room has a real job tomorrow.

At the door he grips Narrator's shirt.

NICK
(whisper)
Take care of Ma.

INT. APARTMENT HALLWAY / ELEVATOR - DAY (WEEKS LATER)

Narrator climbs with a feeling of dread. Apartment empty. No Ma, no purse, no coat. Answering machine blinks.

(SOUND: his key, then machine beep)

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O. ON MACHINE)
Mr. Olson, Milwaukee County Sheriff. We need to talk regarding your family.

NURSE (V.O. ON MACHINE)
Patricia Owens, Frodo Hospital, with an update on your family members.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Old stone, 1898 plaque. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache.

(SOUND: station hubbub, then a quiet interview room)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Your brother Nicholas and your mother Olivia. Hit broadside at Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition. Your mother passed away at the scene. Body at Sacred Heart. Toxicology pending — we think he may have been drunk.

Narrator's hands shake on a styrofoam cup.

(SOUND: his breath stuttering, cup flex)

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Through glass: Nick's wild hair, tubes, machines. Narrator looks at the nurse and turns away.

(SOUND: ICU beeps, curtain rings)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No. I'm really not up to seeing him.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

Ma's empty chair. Afghan folded. Narrator breaks — child-sobbing, pulling hair.

(SOUND: raw crying, then silence)

NARRATOR
Die you fucking jackass. Die.

Sionna holds him. Later, funeral home decisions — moderate casket, Catholic cemetery, priest.

INT. CHURCH / GRAVESIDE - DAY

Few mourners. Bob solemn. Dirt hits the maple lid with a muted thunk. Narrator the only son standing.

(SOUND: thunk of dirt, wind in cemetery trees)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Five days later. Nick sallow, bruised green-black, chest tube, huge leg cast. TV off.

(SOUND: oxygen hiss, distant hall cart)

NICK
(weak)
Hey, bro. I guess I really fucked up. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears on Nick's face. Jack and Cokes before church. Vehicular manslaughter talk. Lawyer. Disability.

NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill her? You son of a bitch.

Narrator looks at the trapped giant in the bed — tubes, no power.

NARRATOR
Think about it.

He walks out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I no longer gave two shits about Nick. I lost my mom. And my brother.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT (MONTHS INTO GRIEF)

Ordinary evening — homework, chips, bed. Last normal night.

(SOUND: pages, shared quiet laugh)

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LATER (NICK'S WAR PATH)

Gray freeze. Nick off the bus, switchblade weight in his pocket. Knocks. Sionna opens a crack.

(SOUND: winter wind, hard knock, cat mew inside)

SIONNA
Nick? I never would have expected you.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Flower candle, vegetable smell. Nick towers. She offers coffee, seat. He refuses.

(SOUND: his pacing, her tight breath)

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. You messed with his head. School. Crazy ideas. He would jump off a bridge if you asked.

SIONNA
Let's talk rationally. What makes you think I'm responsible?

He backs her to the wall. Hand on her throat. Switchblade opens in front of her eyes. Edge to the hollow above her heart.

(SOUND: blade snick, her thin squeak, fridge hum far off)

NICK
Break it off tonight. Cash in a week. Leak one word — I have friends. Parents too. Do we understand?

Front door SLAMS open. Narrator.

NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

Struggle. Blade bites. Red on her shirt. Nick's elbow to Narrator's head. Nick wipes the blade on her and runs.

(SOUND: body impact, wet gasp, door, then 911 on the phone)

Narrator on the floor with her, both hands stacked on the wound near the shoulder, blood everywhere, sirens rising.

NARRATOR
Stay with me. Stay.

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - SEATTLE - DAY (SEVEN YEARS LATER)

Gray Puget Sound light. Ferry. NARRATOR older, filled out, quiet success — owner of Tony's Boy's Pizza chain. Cinnamon roll untouched like a halo. Barista CHRIS somewhere in his future. Cat Bozo at home.

(SOUND: ferry horn distant, restaurant clink, soft rain)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years since my life stopped and became this. Loneliness. Hatred for my brother. I still saw her hair in every crowd.

Memory flashes: pressure on the wound; parents' grateful blame; Sionna in the rocking chair months later, blank ocean eyes.

SIONNA (V.O. MEMORY)
I can't feel me anymore. As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe a break. Mom and Dad's.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She was my life guide. I fell into the void. Then school. Work. Twelve stores. Her name still Sionna in my mouth. I never really dated. Until a dream told me to stop judging. Forgive. Live.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL - VISITING CUBICLE - DAY

Plexiglass. Phones. NICK older, graying, scrawny, bags under sad eyes. Fifteen minutes.

(SOUND: prison doors locking, muffled voices, phone plastic)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
How are you holding up?

NICK
Not a cakewalk. Parole maybe in two.

NARRATOR
I'm here to tell you some things. Just listen. Even though you're my brother, I am no longer interested in seeing you. You're an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down so you could have the big life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.

He breathes. Softens.

NARRATOR
And this is the most important bit. I forgive you. It sets me free. It releases your control forever.

Nick cries openly onto the counter.

NICK
You're so right. I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad about Ma and Sionna. You're more than I ever was. You deserve out there. I deserve in here.

GUARD
Time.

NARRATOR
I want you to get better. I still believe in you. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
What are you doing now?

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn't. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. I'm so proud of you. I love you.

Guard takes Nick away. Narrator sits with the yellow receiver, light.

(SOUND: phone settle, footsteps receding)

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY / AIRPORT TERMINAL - DAY

Rolling green hills then fluorescent boarding area. Narrator with nuts and tea, floating above the throng.

(SOUND: road thrum, then airport PA murmur)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Somewhere she is happy. I have to believe that. Weight of thirty years — gone. No fear, no guilt, no judgment.

He hands the boarding pass. Looks at the name. Nick's proud voice in memory: President Olson.

NARRATOR
(whisper, almost smiling)
That's me. President Peter Olson.

(SOUND: scanner beep, jet engines spooling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
